(g) + 1) field elements. When deg_j(g) = O(1) for all j (the common case), this is O(v) field elements.\nL191: ### What the verifier checks\nL192: \nL193: Per round: one consistency equation (g_j(0) + g_j(1) = previous claim) and a degree bound (deg g_j <= deg_j(g)). After all rounds: one oracle query to g at a random point.\nL194: \nL195: Total verifier time: O(v + sum_j deg_j(g)) + T, where T is the cost of one evaluation of g at a point in F^v.\nL196: ## 2. Our three use cases are one protocol\nL197: \nL198: Thaler's protocol is parameterized by g. Our current three entry points are three instantiations:\nL199: \nL200: Use case  | g  | deg per var  | Ref\nL201: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL202: multilinear_sumcheck  | f_tilde (MLE of evals)  | 1  | §4.1\nL203: inner_product_sumcheck  | f_tilde * g_tilde  | 2  | §4.4\nL204: coefficient_sumcheck  | user-defined  | d  | §4.6\nL205: In all three cases, the protocol is identical — only the prover's round-polynomial computation changes. This motivates a single protocol runner parameterized by a prover trait, not three separate functions.\nL206: ## 3. The prover trait\nL207: \nL208:     /// Prover side of the sum-check protocol (Thaler &sect;4.1).\nL209:     ///\nL210:     /// Implementors define how the round polynomial g_j is computed\nL211:     /// from the prover's internal state. The protocol runner calls\nL212:     /// `round()` once per round, then the caller inspects post-state.\nL213:     pub trait SumcheckProver<F: SumcheckField> {\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nstatesync-gkr/LICENSE at main · Oraclizer/statesync-gkr · GitHub (https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/LICENSE)\nciteturn6view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/Oraclizer/statesync-gkr/blob/main/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 565\nL0: 【0†Skip to content】\nL1: ## Navigation Menu\nL2: \nL3: 【1†Sign in】\nL4: