【17†4.2.3 Complexity of the optimization】\nL28:   6. 【18†5 Removing Nonzeros on the Dense Rows/Columns】\nL29:   7. 【19†6 Experimental Results】\nL30:     1. 【20†6.1 Evaluating Relative Performance of the Variants】\nL31:       1. 【21†6.1.1 Impact on the Encrypted SpMV】\nL32:     2. 【22†6.2 Impact of Dense Row/Column Removal】\nL33:     3. 【23†6.3 The Cost of the Ordering】\nL34:   8. 【24†7 Related Work】\nL35:   9. 【25†8 Conclusion and Future Work】\nL36:   10. 【26†9 Appendix】\nL37:     1. 【27†9.1 Experiments on EigenOrder】\nL38:   11. 【28†Competing interests】\nL39:   12. 【29†Code and Data Availability】\nL40:   13. 【30†Author contributions】\nL41:   14. 【31†Funding】\nL42:   15. 【32†Ethics approval and consent to participate】\nL43:   16. 【33†Consent for publication】\nL44:   17. 【34†References】\nL45: 【35†License: arXiv.org perpetual non-exclusive license†info.arxiv.org】\nL46: \nL47: arXiv:2604.04683v2 [cs.CR] 09 Jul 2026\nL48: # Diagonal Packing for Efficient Homomorphic Sparse Matrix–Vector Multiplication\nL49: Kemal Mutluergil Email: kemal.mutluergil@sabanciuniv.edu Affiliation: Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey    Deniz Elbek Email: deniz.elbek@sabanciuniv.edu Affiliation: Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey Affiliation: VERIM, Center of Excellence in Data Analytics, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey    Kamer Kaya Email: kamer.kaya@sabanciuniv.edu Affiliation: Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey Affiliation: VERIM, Center of Excellence in Data Analytics, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey    Erkay Savaş Email: erkay.savas@sabanciuniv.edu Affiliation: Faculty of Engineering and Natural Sciences, Sabancı University, Istanbul, Turkey\nL50: October 2025\nL51: ###### Abstract\nL52: Homomorphic encryption (HE) enables computation over encrypted data but incurs a substantial overhead. For sparse matrix-vector multiplication, the widely used Halevi-Shoup scheme works over the non-empty diagonals, which may be many due to the irregular nonzero pattern of the matrix. Existing HE matrix-vector methods either use dense diagonal packing, which wastes rotations on empty diagonals, or sparse-coordinate compression, which can expose structural metadata.\nL53: In this work, we instead keep the diagonal-method representation but reorder rows and columns to reduce the number of occupied cyclic diagonals. We formalize this problem as the 2D-diagonal packing problem and provide an integer programming formulation that yields optimal solutions for small instances. For large matrices, we propose practical ordering and iterative-improvement-based optimization heuristics. We also introduce a dense row/column elimination strategy.\nL54: Experiments on 175 real-life matrices show that our ordering–optimization variants can reduce the diagonal count by $5.5\\times$ on average ($45.6\\times$ for one instance). In addition, the dense row/column elimination approach can be useful for cases where the proposed permutation techniques are not sufficient; for instance, in one case, the additional elimination helped to reduce the encrypted multiplication cost by $23.7\\times$ whereas without elimination, the improvement was only $1.9\\times$.\nL55: ###### keywords\nL56: \nL57: Homomorphic encryption; sparse matrix–vector multiplication; diagonal packing; matrix reordering; local search.\nL58: ## 1 Introduction\nL59: \nL60: Homomorphic encryption (HE) allows computations to be performed on ciphertexts whose outputs can be decrypted to the same value as if they were performed on plaintexts. This enables outsourcing the computation for many applications, e.g., neural‑network inference and statistical analysis on sensitive data (【36†Meftah et al., 2021】; 【37†Lee et al., 2022】). However, HE also has a significant computational overhead.\nL61: Let $\\mathbf{A}$ be an $n\\times n$ matrix and $a_{i,j}$ be its entry at the $i$th row and $j$th column for $0\\leq i,j<n$. The matrix–vector multiplication is a fundamental block; for instance, in a neural network, a single layer computes $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ for column vectors $\\mathbf{x}$ and $\\mathbf{y}$. Let $\\mathbf{A}$ be partitioned into $n$ non-overlapping, cyclic diagonals, where the diagonal $k$ for $0\\leq k<n$ contains the entries $a_{i,(k+i)\\bmod n}$ for $0\\leq i<n$.\nL62: If (at least) one entry in a diagonal is nonzero, it is called non-empty. For the encrypted matrix-vector multiplication, Halevi-Shoup packs entries along wrapping diagonals and rotates $\\mathbf{x}$ accordingly before each vector multiplication 【38†Halevi and Shoup (2014)】. Hence, when $\\mathbf{A}$ is dense, $n$ encrypted vector-vector multiplications are performed. When $\\mathbf{A}$ is sparse, these operations can be omitted for empty diagonals.\nL63: Existing HE matrix-vector methods either use dense diagonal packing, which wastes rotations on empty diagonals, or sparse-coordinate compression, which can expose structural metadata. In this work, we instead keep the diagonal-method representation but reorder rows and columns to reduce the number of occupied cyclic diagonals.\nL64: Classical sparse matrix reordering optimizes memory locality; HE SpMV needs a different objective. In Halevi–Shoup-style SpMV, the expensive unit is not a cache miss or a nonzero, but an occupied cyclic diagonal, because each occupied diagonal induces rotations and encrypted vector operations. Therefore, sparse matrices with the same $n$ and number of nonzeros can have different HE costs depending purely on their diagonal support.\nL65: This paper formulates this new layout problem and studies practical algorithms for it.\nL66: More formally, we focus on the following optimization problem: Given a sparse matrix $\\mathbf{A}$, find two permutation matrices ${\\mathbf{P}}$ and ${\\mathbf{Q}}$ such that ${\\mathbf{A}^{\\prime}}={\\mathbf{PAQ^{\\top}}}$ has as few non‑empty cyclic diagonals as possible. The permutations ${\\mathbf{P}}$ and ${\\mathbf{Q}}$ reorder the rows and columns of ${\\mathbf{A}}$, respectively.\nL67: If $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}$ then we have $\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{P}^{\\top}\\mathbf{A^{\\prime}}\\left({\\mathbf{Q}}\\mathbf{x}\\right)$. Hence, one can use ${\\mathbf{A}^{\\prime}}$ instead of ${\\mathbf{A}}$ to reduce the complexity of HE operations.\nL68: Overall, the proposed approach should be viewed as a compiler-level optimization for homomorphic linear algebra: it changes neither the plaintext linear operator nor the HE scheme, but it reduces the number of expensive encrypted rotations and vector multiplications required by the diagonal method.\nL69: The main contributions of the manuscript are summarized as follows:\nL70: \nL71:   * •\nL72: \nL73: We identify occupied cyclic diagonals as the relevant cost metric for Halevi-Shoup-style encrypted SpMV and formulate row-column reordering for this objective as the two-dimensional diagonal packing problem (2DPP).\nL74: \nL75:   * •\nL76: \nL77: Second, we connect 2DPP to classical matrix layout notions, provide a lower bound based on maximum row/column degree, and give a binary ILP formulation that can certify optimal solutions on small instances.\nL78:   * •\nL79: \nL80: Third, we propose a scalable heuristic framework that combines graph-ordering initializers with 2OPT/3OPT local improvement moves evaluated directly on the proposed objective.\nL81: \nL82:   * •\n"
